<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">New York Accords</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1988">1988</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Tripartite Accord (1988) · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-border-war">South African Border War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Accords were signed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> on 22 December 1988 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. They provided for the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a> Resolution 435 and the phased withdrawal of Cuban troops from Angola. Namibia became independent in March 1990, ending South African rule and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-border-war">South African Border War</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1981 Chester Crocker, assistant secretary of state for African affairs under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan">Ronald Reagan</a>, devised a policy of linkage. It tied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s withdrawal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, under Resolution 435, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> to the withdrawal of Cuban troops from Angola. Fidel Castro accepted the proposal in principle on 10 September 1986, and South Africa accepted the principle too, although a majority at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> rejected linking Namibian independence to the Cuban presence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> opened talks in June 1987 while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angolan-civil-war">Angolan Civil War</a> continued. A Cuban delegation joined on 28 January 1988, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> entered the negotiations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> on 3 May. Historians disagree about what brought the parties to the table: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> credits its defence of Cuito Cuanavale, while South Africa points to the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, the effect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> and the cost of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, over 1 billion rand a year. Crocker likened the two delegations to two scorpions in a bottle.</p><p>After meetings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, the parties approved Principles for a Peaceful Settlement in South Western Africa on 20 July and a ceasefire on 8 August 1988; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> refused a request to release <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> as a sign of goodwill. The accord was signed by the foreign ministers Afonso Van-Dunem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, Isidoro Malmierca Peoli of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and Roelof F. Botha of South Africa. Bernt Carlsson, the UN Commissioner for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, was killed on Pan Am Flight 103 on his way to the ceremony.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The accord set a term of 30 months for the Cuban withdrawal, and under it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> was to remove its bases from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and lose the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpla">MPLA</a>'s support; it moved its operations to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> and Uganda. The South African Army had left Angola by 30 August 1988. Cuban troops began to withdraw on 10 January 1989, and the withdrawal was completed a month early, on 25 May 1991. Elections in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> in November 1989 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swapo">SWAPO</a> 57 per cent of the vote, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> kept the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walvis-bay">Walvis Bay</a> for a further 18 months. In Angola the war between the MPLA and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unita">UNITA</a> went on until the death of Jonas Savimbi in 2002.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tripartite Accord (1988)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The fighting around Cuito Cuanavale in 1987–1988 ended without a decisive victory but pushed all sides towards the <b>New York Accords</b> of December 1988, which led to Namibian independence in 1990 and the withdrawal of Cuban troops from Angola.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the 1988 <b>New York Accords</b>, UN-supervised elections were held, and Namibia became independent on 21 March 1990 under President Sam Nujoma.</blockquote><a href="/state/south-west-africa">South West Africa</a></li></ul></section>
